/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccsp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addPlanDetail("ccsp", {"study":{"1":[["What are NIST's five essential characteristics of cloud computing?","On-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity and measured service. ISO/IEC 17788 adds multitenancy."],["How does responsibility for patching change across IaaS, PaaS and SaaS?","In IaaS the customer patches the guest OS and everything above it; in PaaS the provider patches the OS and runtime; in SaaS the provider patches the whole application. Data, identities and access decisions stay with the customer in all three."],["What is the difference between hybrid cloud and multi-cloud?","Hybrid links different deployment models (such as private and public) so workloads can move between them; multi-cloud means using more than one provider, possibly all public."],["Why is a SOC 2 Type II report stronger evidence than a Type I report?","Type I assesses control design at a point in time; Type II also tests that the controls operated effectively over a period, usually six to twelve months."],["Name two security considerations when using a hosted AI model.","Whether prompts and data are retained or used for training and where they are processed; access control and logging for the model endpoint; and AI-specific attacks such as prompt injection."]],"2":[["List the phases of the cloud secure data lifecycle and where classification belongs.","Create, store, use, share, archive, destroy. Classification should happen at create."],["When would you choose tokenization over encryption?","When you want systems to handle a surrogate with no mathematical link to the real value, for example to reduce PCI DSS scope; encrypted values are still sensitive data because they can be decrypted with the key."],["Compare BYOK and HYOK.","BYOK imports a customer-generated key into the provider's KMS, where the provider's service uses it. HYOK keeps the key in the customer's own key service outside the provider, giving more control but adding latency and dependency."],["Why is crypto-shredding the preferred sanitization method in public cloud?","The customer cannot physically destroy or verify wiping of shared media; destroying all copies of the keys makes every encrypted copy unreadable."],["What does a legal hold do to retention schedules?","It overrides them: data relevant to anticipated or actual litigation must be preserved until counsel releases the hold, even if its retention period has ended."]],"3":[["Describe the four Uptime Institute tiers in one phrase each.","Tier I basic capacity, Tier II redundant components, Tier III concurrently maintainable, Tier IV fault tolerant."],["What is the difference between a security group and a network ACL?","Security groups are stateful and attach to instances or interfaces; network ACLs are usually stateless, attach to subnets and support explicit deny rules."],["What is the most common cause of cloud data exposure and how is it reduced?","Customer misconfiguration; reduce it with secure defaults, infrastructure as code with policy checks, and continuous posture scanning."],["How do you choose a DR strategy in the cloud?","Use the BIA's RTO and RPO (within the MTD) and pick the least expensive strategy that meets them, from backup and restore to pilot light, warm standby or active-active, remembering the provider itself can fail."]],"4":[["Name the STRIDE categories and the property each violates.","Spoofing (authentication), tampering (integrity), repudiation (non-repudiation), information disclosure (confidentiality), denial of service (availability), elevation of privilege (authorization)."],["Compare SAST, DAST, IAST and SCA.","SAST analyzes code without running it; DAST attacks a running app from outside; IAST uses an agent inside a running app during tests; SCA inventories third-party components and their known vulnerabilities."],["Why is SSRF especially dangerous in the cloud?","It can make a server call the instance metadata service and steal temporary credentials for the workload's identity."],["What is the difference between OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect and SAML?","OAuth 2.0 is for delegated authorization to APIs; OpenID Connect adds authentication on top of OAuth; SAML is an XML standard for enterprise SSO assertions."]],"5":[["What should you do first with a compromised IaaS instance?","Isolate it (for example with a restrictive security group) without shutting it down, then capture memory and snapshot disks with hashes and chain of custody before remediation."],["What is the difference between incident management and problem management?","Incident management restores service as fast as possible; problem management finds and removes the root cause so incidents do not recur."],["What does DNSSEC protect and what does it not?","It protects integrity and authenticity of DNS responses against spoofing and cache poisoning; it does not encrypt queries."],["Name one benefit and one limit of ML anomaly detection in a SOC.","Benefit: it can detect new attacks that match no signature. Limit: false positives when behavior changes and false negatives when attackers blend into the baseline."]],"6":[["Who is usually the controller and who the processor when a company uses SaaS for customer data?","The company is the controller (it decides purposes and means); the SaaS provider is the processor. Accountability stays with the controller."],["Compare SOC 1, SOC 2 and SOC 3.","SOC 1 covers controls relevant to financial reporting; SOC 2 covers the trust services criteria in detail for informed users; SOC 3 is a general-use summary of SOC 2 without detailed results."],["Which contract terms protect a customer against lock-in?","Exit and portability terms: data export format and timing, assistance during transition, and certified deletion afterwards."],["What does a provider's PCI DSS attestation not cover?","Services outside its listed scope and the customer's own configurations, applications and processes, which need their own compliance evidence."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Cloud concepts, architecture & design","w":17,"topics":["Cloud computing concepts: NIST definitions, essential characteristics and roles (customer, provider, partner, broker, regulator)","Cloud reference architecture: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS service models and cloud service capabilities","Cloud deployment models: public, private, community, hybrid and multi-cloud","Shared responsibility model across service models","Related technologies: containers, serverless, edge computing, confidential computing, DevSecOps and quantum","AI and machine learning in the cloud: service types, use cases and security considerations","Security concepts for cloud computing: cryptography and key management, identity, data and media sanitization, network security, virtualization security","Design principles of secure cloud computing: secure data lifecycle, cloud-based BC/DR, BIA, cost-benefit and security patterns","Evaluating cloud service providers: ISO/IEC 27017, CSA STAR, SOC 2, Common Criteria and FIPS 140-3"],"notes":["Domain 1 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: cloud computing concepts, reference architecture, AI and ML in the cloud, security concepts, secure design principles, evaluating providers"],"labs":["Build a shared responsibility matrix for one workload (for example a customer database) delivered as IaaS, PaaS and SaaS: list 12 tasks such as patching, identity, key management, logging and physical security, and mark provider, customer or shared for each model.","Download a major provider's free SOC 3 report or ISO/IEC 27001 certificate and look up its CSA STAR registry entry; record the scope (services, regions, dates) and list three questions you would still ask before storing regulated data there.","Write a one-page business impact analysis for a small online shop's order system: set MTD, RTO and RPO, then choose the cheapest DR pattern (backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby or active-active) that meets them and justify it."]},{"id":2,"name":"Cloud data security","w":20,"topics":["Cloud data concepts: cloud data lifecycle phases, data dispersion and data flows","Cloud data storage architectures: storage types (ephemeral, raw, long-term, object, volume, database) and threats to storage","Data security technologies: encryption and key management, hashing, data obfuscation (masking, anonymization), tokenization","Data loss prevention (DLP) in the cloud","Keys, secrets and certificates management: KMS, HSM, BYOK and HYOK","Data discovery: structured, semi-structured and unstructured data, and data location","Data classification: policies, data mapping and data labeling","Information rights management (IRM): objectives, provisioning, access models and tools","Data retention, deletion and archiving policies, including legal hold and crypto-shredding","Auditability, traceability and accountability of data events","Protecting AI and ML data: training data integrity, data poisoning, model and prompt security"],"notes":["Domain 2 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: data lifecycle, storage architectures, data security technologies, discovery, classification, IRM, retention and deletion, auditability, protecting AI and ML data"],"labs":["In a free-tier cloud account, create an object storage bucket, confirm the account-level public access block is on, write a bucket policy that lets only one role read objects and denies non-TLS requests, and enable default encryption with a customer-managed key.","Create a key in a free-tier cloud key management service, encrypt and decrypt a small file with the CLI, find the key-usage events in the audit log, then schedule the key for deletion and explain how that would crypto-shred any data it protected.","Draft a four-level data classification scheme and map five sample data types (card numbers, HR records, marketing copy, source code, system logs) to a level, an approved storage location and one protection technique (encryption, masking, tokenization or IRM)."]},{"id":3,"name":"Cloud platform & infrastructure security","w":17,"topics":["Cloud infrastructure components: physical environment, network and communications, compute, virtualization, storage and management plane","Secure data center design: logical design, physical design, environmental design and tier levels","Analyzing risks to cloud infrastructure and platforms: virtualization risks, countermeasures and threats","Security controls: physical and environmental protection, system and communication protection, identification and authentication","Securing the management plane and hypervisors: type 1 vs type 2, VM escape and isolation","Network security in the cloud: virtual networks, security groups, microsegmentation, zero trust and VPNs","Business continuity and disaster recovery in the cloud: strategy, RTO/RPO, plan creation and testing","Audit mechanisms: log collection, correlation and packet capture in cloud environments"],"notes":["Domain 3 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: infrastructure components, secure data center design, infrastructure risks, security controls, BC/DR planning"],"labs":["In a free tier, create a virtual network with a public and a private subnet, place a small test VM in the private subnet, and write security group rules so only the load balancer's security group can reach it on port 443 and no SSH is open to the internet.","Enable flow logs on a lab virtual network, generate some allowed and blocked traffic, then read the log records and identify source, destination, port and whether each connection was accepted or rejected.","Write a risk register for a small IaaS environment with five infrastructure risks (misconfiguration, VM sprawl, management plane compromise, provider region outage, snapshot leakage), each with likelihood, impact, owner and treatment."]},{"id":4,"name":"Cloud application security","w":16,"topics":["Training and awareness for application security: cloud development basics and common pitfalls","Secure software development lifecycle (SDLC): phases, methodologies and threat modeling (STRIDE, DREAD, PASTA, ATASM)","Common cloud vulnerabilities: OWASP Top 10 and SANS/CWE Top 25","Applying the SDLC in cloud: secure coding, ASVS and software configuration management","Cloud software assurance and validation: functional and non-functional testing, SAST, DAST, IAST, SCA, abuse cases","Using verified secure software: approved APIs, supply chain management, third-party and open-source components","Specifics of cloud application architecture: WAF, XML gateways, API gateways, database activity monitoring, cryptography, sandboxing, app virtualization","Identity and access management solutions: federated identity, identity providers, SSO, MFA, CASB and secrets management"],"notes":["Domain 4 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: secure SDLC and threat modeling, common vulnerabilities, software assurance and testing, verified software and supply chain, application architecture, IAM solutions"],"labs":["Run a STRIDE threat model on a simple serverless upload API (API gateway, function, object storage): draw the data flow with trust boundaries, list at least one threat per STRIDE category and a mitigation for each.","Scan a small open-source project with a free software composition analysis tool (for example OWASP Dependency-Check) and a free SAST tool (for example Semgrep), then triage the top three findings and decide which to fix first.","Run OWASP Juice Shop locally, use OWASP ZAP to perform a baseline DAST scan against it, and map three findings to OWASP Top 10 categories with the code or configuration fix you would require."]},{"id":5,"name":"Cloud security operations","w":17,"topics":["Building and implementing physical and logical infrastructure: hardware security (TPM, HSM), virtualization toolsets and guest OS installation","Operating and maintaining physical and logical infrastructure: access controls for local and remote access, secure network configuration (VLAN, TLS, DHCP, DNSSEC, VPN)","Hardening, patch management and infrastructure as code","Availability, clustering, performance and capacity monitoring, and backup and restore of the host and guest OS","Implementing operational controls and standards: ITIL and ISO/IEC 20000-1 processes (change, configuration, incident, problem, release, deployment)","Supporting digital forensics: forensic data collection methodologies, evidence management, chain of custody","Communicating with relevant parties: customers, vendors, partners, regulators and other stakeholders","Security operations: SOC, intelligent monitoring of security controls, log capture and analysis (SIEM), incident management and vulnerability assessments","Using AI and ML in security operations: anomaly detection, automation and their limits"],"notes":["Domain 5 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: building and operating infrastructure, hardening and patching, availability and backup, ITSM controls, forensics, communication, security operations, AI and ML for threat detection"],"labs":["Turn on management-plane audit logging in a free-tier account (for example AWS CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log or Google Cloud Audit Logs), send it to a separate storage location, create and delete a test resource, and find both events with their caller identity.","Write a golden-image checklist for a Linux VM based on the CIS Benchmark (patching, SSH settings, logging, unused services, time sync), apply five items to a lab VM and record before-and-after evidence.","Run a tabletop exercise for a leaked cloud access key: write the detection signal, containment steps, evidence to preserve with chain of custody, and who must be told inside and outside the company and when."]},{"id":6,"name":"Legal, risk & compliance","w":13,"topics":["Legal requirements and unique risks in the cloud: conflicting international law, eDiscovery (ISO/IEC 27050, CSA guidance) and forensic requirements","Privacy issues: contractual vs regulated private data, country-specific laws (GDPR, HIPAA, GLBA), jurisdictional differences and privacy impact assessments","Audit process, methodologies and adaptations for cloud: internal vs external audit, assurance challenges of virtualization, SOC reports, gap analysis, audit planning","Implications of cloud for enterprise risk management: data owner/controller vs custodian/processor, regulatory transparency, risk treatment","Risk frameworks and metrics: ISO/IEC 31000, ENISA cloud risk guidance, NIST SP 800-37, and assessing a provider's risk management","Outsourcing and cloud contract design: business requirements (SLA, MSA, SOW), vendor management and supply chain management (ISO/IEC 27036)","Policies for cloud: organizational and functional policies, and cloud computing policies","AI regulation and ethics in the cloud: regulatory requirements, bias, transparency and accountability","Specialized compliance requirements: PCI DSS, FedRAMP, HIPAA, NERC CIP and certification scope"],"notes":["Domain 6 of the outline effective Aug 1, 2026: legal requirements and eDiscovery, privacy, audit, enterprise risk management, contracts and supply chain, policies, AI regulation and ethics, specialized compliance"],"labs":["Pick a SaaS product you use and read its data processing agreement and security documentation: identify controller and processor roles, subprocessors, data locations, breach notification timing and exit and deletion terms.","Complete a short privacy impact assessment for moving an HR onboarding form to a SaaS tool: data collected, purpose, data flows, retention, risks to individuals and three mitigations.","Compare the SLAs two providers publish for the same service type (availability target, measurement, service credits, exclusions) and list the contract terms you would still negotiate, such as breach notice, audit reports and data export."]}]});
